Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.680
RYAN: Today, we're
talking about cloud summit

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.910
series, data analytics
skills, and IAM for Cloud SQL.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:07.510
I'm Ryan.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.130
And welcome to
this week in cloud,

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.280
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.550
Register for our 2022
Google Cloud Summit Series

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.680
and be among the first to
learn about new solutions

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.340
across data, machine learning,
collaboration, security,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.320
sustainability, and more.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.500
You'll hear from experts,
explore customer perspectives,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.670
engage with interactive demos,
and gain valuable insights

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.980
to help you accelerate your
business transformation.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.673
Our first event is
coming up in April.

00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:37.090
And you'll want to bookmark
the Google Cloud Summit Series

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.100
website for all the updates.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.100
These days, companies must
deliver simple experiences

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.540
to be successful.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.430
For many cloud
providers, this means

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.740
enabling tightly integrated
data offerings that

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.530
simplify the data delivery
process without losing

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.220
sight of the sophisticated needs
of the modern data consumer.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.250
It begs the question, to stay
ahead of data cloud market

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.460
trends in 2022, should
data practitioners

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.920
invest their time in
specializing their data cloud

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.190
skill sets or instead invest
their time generalizing

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.080
their data cloud skillsets?

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.350
Check out the article
to learn more.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.810
If you're responsible for
administering Cloud SQL

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.270
instances, securing
your databases

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.580
is one of your top priorities.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.610
However, as your number of users
on your SQL instances grows,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.180
you may find yourself challenged
to keep access locked down.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.190
We're happy to announce
that Cloud SQL support

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:31.550
for IAM conditions is
now generally available.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.060
IAM conditions are part of Cloud
Identity and Access Management,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.570
or IAM, the service that Cloud
SQL and other Google Cloud

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:42.530
services rely on for determining
access to cloud resources.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.187
Check out the blog
for the full post.

00:01:45.187 --> 00:01:47.270
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.950
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.350
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.130
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.600
We'll see you in the cloud.

